<p>Still 9 verified lenders. New Silver, Stormfield, and CoreVest still don't show a fix-and-flip rate we can confirm on their own sites, so they stay off the table. Jaken's page is now about three months old; we'll keep flagging it until they update it.</p>
<p>Full table, updated daily: <a href="{pre}rates.html">wefliphousesllc.com/rates.html</a></p>
<h2>New on the site: the BRRRR calculator</h2>
<p>Buy, rehab with hard money, rent it, refinance into a rental loan. The new calculator shows how much cash stays stuck in the deal after the refi, your monthly cash flow, cash-on-cash return, and the DSCR a rental lender will look at.</p>
<p><a href="{pre}brrrr-calculator.html">Run your BRRRR numbers</a></p>
<h2>Deal breakdown: DSCR says yes, cash flow says no (EXAMPLE numbers)</h2>
<p>These are made-up numbers to show the math. They aren't from a real property or any lender.</p>
<p>Buy for $120,000, $3,000 closing, $35,000 rehab. Hard money covers 90% of purchase and all of the rehab ($143,000) at 10% interest-only, 2 points + $1,500 in fees. Refinance after 6 months at 75% of a $200,000 ARV, 7.5% for 30 years. Rent is $1,700.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Step</th>
<th>Amount</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Cash you put in (down payment, closing, interest, points, holding)</td>
<td>$29,510</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>New refi loan (75% of $200,000)</td>
<td>$150,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Pay off hard money</td>
<td>−$143,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Refi closing costs (3%)</td>
<td>−$4,500</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Cash back at refi</strong></td>
<td><strong>$2,500</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Cash left in the deal</strong></td>
<td><strong>$27,010</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Now the monthly side:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Monthly</th>
<th>Amount</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Rent</td>
<td>$1,700</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Principal + interest</td>
<td>−$1,049</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Taxes + insurance</td>
<td>−$300</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Vacancy, repairs, management (23% of rent)</td>
<td>−$391</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Cash flow</strong></td>
<td><strong>−$40</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The DSCR here is $1,700 ÷ $1,349 = <strong>1.26</strong>. Plenty of DSCR lenders would be happy with that, because their formula only counts principal, interest, taxes, insurance, and HOA. It ignores vacancy, repairs, and management. Once you set money aside for those, this property costs you about $40 a month, with $27,000 of your cash still tied up in it.</p>
<p>The lender's ratio tells you if they'll make the loan. It doesn't tell you if you should take it. Try your own deal with a lower appraisal and a higher refi rate before you buy.</p>
<h2>Quick tip: ask about seasoning before you buy</h2>
<p>Many rental lenders won't refinance on the new appraised value until you've owned the property for a set number of months. If yours wants more time than your rehab takes, you're paying hard money interest while you wait. Ask the refi lender for their seasoning rule up front, and plug that number into "months until refinance."</p>
